# Job control through the serial line (TASK-18 step 9, ADR-0158). Ctrl-C,
# Ctrl-\ and Ctrl-Z reach only the job in front, every process of it
# (INV-0158-1); a job in the background reads no key (INV-0158-2); exit
# ends nothing the first time and every job the second (INV-0158-3).
# `requests NAME` says each request it takes, numbered, on its err; the two
# elements of a pipeline start in either order, so a pause and `o` check
# them.
! panicked
! first process ended
# The background job takes one interrupt, from kill, and nothing else.
! a: interrupt 2
! b: interrupt 2
! a: quit
! b: quit
! c: interrupt 3
! d: interrupt 3
! c: quit 2
! d: quit 2
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n/>
# A job of two processes in the background, and one in front.
> requests a | requests b &\r
< [1] requests a | requests b
< \r\n/>
~ 800 zzz
o a: ready
o b: ready
> requests c | requests d\r
~ 800 zzz
o c: ready
o d: ready
# Ctrl-C: an interrupt to both in front; a second later the shell offers
# the kill.
> \x03
< ^C
< (Ctrl-C again to kill it)
o c: interrupt 1
o d: interrupt 1
# Ctrl-Z stops the job in front, and the prompt comes back.
> \x1a
< ^Z
< [2] stopped: requests c | requests d
< \r\n/>
# jobs reads the stopped state from the kernel (SUSPENDED on the job).
> jobs\r
< [1]  running   requests a | requests b
< [2]  stopped   requests c | requests d
< \r\n/>
> bg\r
< [2] requests c | requests d &
< \r\n/>
> jobs\r
< [2]  running   requests c | requests d
< \r\n/>
# fg: the job in front again; Ctrl-\ and Ctrl-C reach both of it.
> fg %2\r
< requests c | requests d
> \x1c
< ^\\
~ 600 zzz
o c: quit 1
o d: quit 1
> \x03
< ^C
< (Ctrl-C again to kill it)
o c: interrupt 2
o d: interrupt 2
# The second Ctrl-C, once offered, kills.
> \x03
< ^C
< requests c | requests d: killed
< \r\n/>
# The background job took none of those: its first interrupt is kill's.
> kill --interrupt %1\r
< [1] still running; kill --kill %1 ends it
< \r\n/>
o a: interrupt 1
o b: interrupt 1
# INV-0158-2: a background reader finds its input at its end at once, while
# keys are typed at the prompt; they stay the prompt's.
> requests --read e &\rtyped
< e: end of input after 0 bytes
< [2] done: requests --read e
< /> typed
> \x15jobs\r
< [1]  running   requests a | requests b
x [2]
< \r\n/>
# INV-0158-3: exit with a running and a stopped job ends nothing...
> requests f\r
< f: ready
> \x1a
< [2] stopped: requests f
< \r\n/>
> exit\r
< 2 jobs are still running; exit again to end them
< \r\n/>
> jobs\r
< [1]  running   requests a | requests b
< [2]  stopped   requests f
< \r\n/>
# ...anything between counts again, and the second exit in a row ends both.
> exit\r
< 2 jobs are still running; exit again to end them
< \r\n/>
> exit\r
< init: shell ended: ExitRecord { code: 0, reason: Exited
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n/>
> jobs\r
x [1]
< \r\n/>
x power off
> poweroff\r
